A||||1||MooCow||Ford Ranger clutch help!||||11||
Z||000000||MooCow||12-10-2000||11:03 PM||||I have a 91 Ford ranger V6 3.0.  It has right now 93,400 miles on her.  The clutch is starting to feel funny.  I can push it in about 1/3 of the way as I am driving and nothing happens.  I went to get an oil change and they filled everything up and the clutch felt tight.  Now after 1,000 miles it is going back to the way I described up above.  So does this mean my clutch is going out?  How do I tell?<P>                                 Thanks Ken||||reg||11
Z||000001||Gonzo_442||12-13-2000||01:34 AM||||Funny? You mean it feels like there is no resistance, and it won't engage the clutch?<P>If you have the ticket (invoice, whatever) from what they did, look on it to see if it says anything about the clutch.<P>The 91 still uses a hydraulic clutch does it not? Assuming it does...<P>Since it went back to normal after they 'filled everything up' chances are they filled the clutch fluid too.<P>i'm working off an 87 here, the 91 is probably similar...<BR>Pop the hood, near the master brake cylinder (close to the firewall, driver's side) is a small white plastic piece, about the size of a baby food jar. It's got a little hose running out of the bottom. The hose runs down a little, and connects to a metal cylinder, about as big around as your thumb, and about four inches long. Mounted end on the firewall, sticking out into the engine compartment. That is the slave cylinder.<BR>My best guess is you have a leak somewhere along there. Open the top of the clutch fluid reservoir, see if there is fluid in there. If not, fill it. (factory spec, or i use regular brake fluid, ymmv).<BR>Step on the clutch a few times, slowly.<BR>If it returns to normal that's your problem (a leak). Best way to be sure is to clean the area well, fill the reservoir, and when it goes back to feeling funny, check to see if fluid leaked out.<BR>It is not unheard of (pretty common actually) for the seals in the slave cylinder to go bad. But check the lines as well. If it were the cylinder, i would say expect around $50 (parts and labor) to have a garage do it. Look into replacing it soon. If a seal suddenly fails (instead of just leaks), you will find yourself with no clutch at all, not a real good thing.<BR>Side note: if you recently had clutch work done (i.e. replaced it) they should have replaced the cylinder too. Though some places don't to keep cost of repair down.<P>I doubt the clutch is going bad. This is all an educated guess though, kind of hard to do long distance diagnostics  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>hope this helps and as always, ymmv  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>--<BR>g||||reg||1
